CredScope Debian source packaging notes
========================================

Go dependency graph and packaging strategy
-------------------------------------------

CredScope's actual build-time Go dependency graph (verified with
`go list -deps ./cmd/credscope`, not assumed) is small:

  - github.com/spf13/cobra      v1.10.2  (Apache-2.0)
  - github.com/spf13/pflag      v1.0.9   (BSD-3-Clause)
  - github.com/inconshreveable/mousetrap v1.1.0 (Apache-2.0)
  - gopkg.in/yaml.v3            v3.0.1   (MIT and Apache-2.0)

(cobra also pulls github.com/cpuguy83/go-md2man/v2, github.com/russross/
blackfriday/v2, and gopkg.in/check.v1 into go.sum, but those are cobra's
own doc-generation and test dependencies; CredScope does not import
cobra's `doc` package, so they are not compiled into the credscope
binary.)

All four real dependencies are packaged in Debian/Kali, confirmed inside
a real kali-rolling container (`apt-cache policy`), not assumed:

  - golang-github-spf13-cobra-dev               1.10.2-1
  - golang-github-spf13-pflag-dev                1.0.10-1
  - golang-github-inconshreveable-mousetrap-dev  1.1.0-1
  - golang-gopkg-yaml.v3-dev                     3.0.4-3

Earlier packaging revisions of CredScope imported go.yaml.in/yaml/v3 (the
goyaml.in project's continuation of the same API under a new module
path), which was NOT packaged in Debian/Kali, forcing full vendoring.
CredScope's own Go import was switched from go.yaml.in/yaml/v3 to the
already-packaged gopkg.in/yaml.v3 specifically to remove that need (the
two are API-compatible; goyaml.in v3 is an explicit continuation of the
same code). There is now no vendor/ directory and no dependency that
needs vendoring.

Build strategy: dh-golang against system libraries
----------------------------------------------------

With every real dependency packaged, debian/rules uses the dh-golang
buildsystem (`dh $@ --buildsystem=golang --with=golang`, confirmed
working end-to-end in a real kali-rolling container, not assumed):

  - `dh_auto_configure --buildsystem=golang` assembles a temporary GOPATH
    under `_build/`, copying CredScope's own source to
    `_build/src/github.com/Bavlik/CredScope` and symlinking every
    installed golang-*-dev package's source from `/usr/share/gocode/src/`.
  - This is legacy GOPATH mode (`GO111MODULE=off`): it does not read
    go.mod/go.sum at all, and therefore never needs network access or a
    module proxy. It builds directly against whatever version of each
    dependency the installed Debian package provides.
  - `override_dh_auto_build` calls `dh_auto_build --buildsystem=golang --
    -ldflags "..."` so the built binary still gets the same
    `main.version`/`main.commit`/`main.date` metadata CredScope's
    GoReleaser release build and root_test.go expect.
  - `override_dh_auto_install` installs only `_build/bin/credscope` to
    `/usr/bin/credscope`. dh-golang's default install() would also copy
    `_build/bin/credscope-action` and `_build/bin/generate-examples`
    (CredScope's other, non-CLI-facing build products) and the entire Go
    source tree to `/usr/share/gocode/src/...` (dh-golang's default
    behavior for library packages); CredScope ships only the one CLI
    binary, so both are skipped explicitly.
  - `dh_golang` (from the `dh-golang` Build-Depends, part of the standard
    `--with=golang` sequence) generates the `misc:Static-Built-Using`
    substvar automatically from the real Build-Depends versions used,
    resolving the previous `missing-static-built-using-field-for-golang-
    package` lintian informational note.

Because the build now happens entirely in GOPATH mode against installed
Debian packages, `go.mod`/`go.sum` are present in the source tree (for
upstream's normal `go build`/`go test` outside of Debian packaging, and
so `go mod verify`/`go vet` keep working for everyone else) but are not
consulted by the Debian build at all.

Test scope during the build
-----------------------------

`override_dh_auto_test` runs `go test` (via `dh_auto_test
--buildsystem=golang`) over every package except
internal/repositoryquality, which tests CredScope's own GitHub-facing
CI/release artifacts (GitHub Actions workflows, WinGet manifests, README
wording) rather than the credscope binary, and some of whose tests shell
out to `pwsh` to validate Windows WinGet packaging scripts -- an
irrelevant, heavy dependency for a Debian package build. Every package
that tests actual credscope behavior still runs, including the packages
that changed with the yaml import switch (internal/config,
internal/parsers/compose, internal/parsers/githubactions,
internal/parsers/yamlsafe).

Getting the orig tarball
--------------------------

Because there is no vendoring, the .orig tarball is now just CredScope's
own tagged source, unmodified -- no repacking step is required (unlike
the earlier vendored revision of this packaging). debian/watch points at
GitHub's tag tarballs directly. To generate `../credscope_<version>.orig.
tar.gz` for a real upload:

    uscan --download-current-version --destdir=..

or, equivalently and offline once the plain upstream tag tarball has
already been fetched once:

    git archive --format=tar.gz --prefix=credscope-<version>/ \
      v<version> > ../credscope_<version>.orig.tar.gz

Either way, the resulting .orig tarball does not need vendor/ and the
Debian build never downloads anything: everything it needs comes from
the golang-*-dev Build-Depends already installed in the build chroot.
